    "jays": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Blue Jays",
      "league": "MLB",
      "record": "17-21",
      "detail": "3rd in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "Dylan Cease throttled the Angels into submission Friday night - ten strikeouts in seven scoreless innings, a masterclass in command that felt like a release valve for a franchise gasping for air. <strong>Toronto snapped a four-game losing streak with a 2-0 shutout</strong>, Kazuma Okamoto's third-inning RBI single and Ernie Clement's sacrifice fly providing just enough offense in a game where pitching was the only currency that mattered. It was the kind of win that doesn't erase the damage - the Blue Jays remain 17-21, eight and a half games behind New York in the AL East, clinging to third place like a climber on a crumbling ledge - but it matters. One win doesn't resurrect a season, but one dominant start by your ace whispers that maybe, just maybe, the machinery still works. Cease has now reached double digits in strikeouts three times in eight starts; he's become the only reliable heartbeat in a lineup that's been flatlined for weeks. <strong>Toronto hosts Los Angeles again Saturday at 2:07 p.m. ET, a chance to build momentum before the season's narrative gets written in permanent ink.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated May 9, 2026"
      },
