      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Commanders",
        "body": "The draft dust settles in Ashburn, and Dan Quinn's Commanders - fresh off a <strong>5-12 slog that landed them third in the NFC East, six games back</strong> - emerged with a rookie haul screaming rebuild with bite. They pounced on versatile RB <strong>Omarion Hampton</strong> from North Carolina at No. 40 overall in Round 2, a bruising 1,000-yard grinder whose north-south style slots perfectly into Kliff Kingsbury's gap-scheme offense, poised to spell Brian Robinson and push for 150 carries as a rookie. Round 3 brought Georgia edge rusher <strong>Jalon Walker</strong> at No. 75, a heat-seeking missile with 8.5 sacks last fall, injecting speed off the edge where the pass rush ranked dead last; he's Day 1 depth behind sweat-soaked vets like Clelin Ferrell. Later gems included Oklahoma WR <strong>Nic Anderson</strong> (Round 5, No. 145), a deep threat to stretch fields for Jayden Daniels, and UDFAs like Auburn DT <strong>Khalan Laborn</strong>, who bolted 4.65 and could crack the D-line rotation. No more excuses - this class fortifies the trenches, fits the scheme like a glove. After last January's fade, the 5-12 bruise fades. <strong>Watch OTAs in June: Hampton's workload and Walker's snaps will signal if Quinn's reloaded or just re-shuffled.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated May 9, 2026"
      },
